---
/**
 * foodcraft / HowBM — "Tahanan Cafe" HOW IT WORKS (design element
 * data-component="HOW-IT-WORKS").
 *
 * ROLE: the page's one procedural block — how an order is actually placed at a
 * café that has no ordering system. It sits between the gallery and the
 * reviews, on the same white ground as everything around it, and it is the
 * clearest example of this design's grid rule: THE GRID IS DRAWN WITH BORDERS,
 * NOT GAPS. A hairline runs across the top of the row and every cell carries a
 * right-hand and a bottom hairline, so the three steps read as ruled columns of
 * a page rather than as three cards.
 *
 * DESIGN VALUES, lifted verbatim from "Tahanan Cafe Landing.dc.html" line 151:
 *   section    max-width 1160 / padding 84px 30px 0          → .wrap + the
 *              wrapper's shared `section` rule (see NOT RENDERED)
 *   header     text-align center, margin-bottom 44px
 *   eyebrow    10px / .34em caps / #7A4E2D 700, on a
 *              1px #E0D3C6 underline, padding-bottom 9px     → .kick, UNTOUCHED
 *   h2         Libre Caslon Text 400, clamp(29px,3.8vw,50px)
 *              /1.14, margin .26em 0 0, #241B14, NO cap      → .h2 + .hbm-h2
 *   emphasis   real <em> italic in #7A4E2D                   → wrapper .h2 em
 *   grid       repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)), NO gap,
 *              border-top 1px #EDE6DF                        → --line
 *   cell       padding 32px 30px,
 *              border-right + border-bottom 1px #EDE6DF      → --line
 *   numeral    Libre Caslon Text ITALIC 17px #7A4E2D         → .fig + .hbm-n
 *   h3         Libre Caslon Text 400, 24px,
 *              margin 13px 0 9px, #241B14
 *   body       15px / 1.8 / #5C5147, margin 0                → .body
 *   action     centred, margin-top 38px, btnBrown =
 *              btn + accent fill + #FFFFFF label             → .btn.btn--fill
 * Not one hex is written in this file (R1). btnBrown is character-for-character
 * the wrapper's `.btn` base plus `.btn--fill`, so this file adds NO button rule
 * at all beyond the full-width, wrapping label at 520px.
 *
 * EDITABLE HOOKS (every one declared in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts,
 * group id "how" / group id "hero" — a data-field on any other path is a dead
 * hook that no sidebar input owns)
 *   how.tag                  — the underlined walnut eyebrow
 *   how.headline             — fed with set:html, so an owner can place the
 *                              design's signature italic walnut emphasis inside
 *                              their OWN sentence. Guarded: derive-content-
 *                              defaults supplies no how.headline, so an unset
 *                              value must draw nothing rather than open a gap
 *   how.steps.${i}.title     — original index, see LIST INDEXES
 *   how.steps.${i}.body      — canonical key, spelled in the hook even when the
 *                              stored value arrived as .desc / .description, so
 *                              the first save heals the row onto the schema key
 *   hero.cta1.text           — the walnut button's label
 *   hero.cta1.href           — its target. Deliberately the page's ONE primary
 *                              action reused rather than a new field: the schema
 *                              declares hero.cta1.text as a `link` FieldSpec
 *                              whose hrefPath is hero.cta1.href, so both halves
 *                              are owned by a real sidebar input. The design
 *                              reuses its own contactUrl/contactLabel in exactly
 *                              this slot
 *
 * DERIVED, not content:
 *   THE NUMERAL, and this is the decision a reader is most likely to think is an
 *   oversight. The design prints word ordinals — "One", "Two", "Three" — from
 *   its own `st.n` demo array. Those are the DESIGNER filling in their own
 *   template, and they are three English words this component would be putting
 *   on a Filipino owner's page (R2). What the design SPECIFIES is the treatment:
 *   Libre Caslon Text, real italic, 17px, walnut, sitting above the title. That
 *   treatment is reproduced exactly and the string is derived — a zero-padded
 *   DISPLAY index, the same numeral form the very same design file uses for its
 *   CREDENTIALS column. It is aria-hidden and carries no data-field, so it can
 *   never become a content key an owner has to maintain. The list is a real
 *   <ol>, so the ordinal reaches assistive tech through the markup instead.
 *
 *   THE TRACK COUNT. `--cols` follows the DATA, capped at the design's three.
 *   The design writes repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)) over its own three-row demo
 *   array; hardcoding three under two filled steps would leave the row's top
 *   hairline running on across a third of the page with no cell beneath it and
 *   the bottom rule stopping short — a broken table rather than an editorial
 *   one, because this grid is drawn with BORDERS and a missing cell takes its
 *   rules with it. At three steps or more the computed value is
 *   character-for-character the design's. Same idiom as TrustBM's `--cols`.
 *
 *   THE LIST ROLE. The <ol> carries role="list", because `list-style: none` is
 *   exactly what makes Safari/VoiceOver drop list semantics — and the ordinal
 *   reaching assistive tech through the markup is the entire reason the visible
 *   numeral is aria-hidden. Same idiom as AreaBM's <ul>.
 *
 * ANTI-FABRICATION (R2). Not one word here is written by this file. The design's
 * own steps — "Message us the drinks and a pickup time", "Mornings before nine
 * we reply fastest", "Made to hit your minute" — are a reply-time promise and a
 * punctuality guarantee made on behalf of a business nobody has spoken to, so
 * none of them ships. A café that has said nothing about its process gets no
 * process section at all: blank rows are dropped and an all-blank list removes
 * the whole <section>.
 *
 * SECTION GATE (R5) = rows.length, the BODY content. It cannot count how.tag,
 * because lib/derive-content-defaults.ts hard-codes how.tag to 'How it works' on
 * EVERY site — a gate that counted it would ship an eyebrow, a headline and a
 * bare top hairline over an empty grid on every site that never filled the steps
 * in. The CTA is not counted either: a section whose only content is a button is
 * a heading over nothing.
 *
 * LIST INDEXES (R6). data-field carries the ORIGINAL array index; the numeral
 * carries the DISPLAY index. Dropping a blank row 2 therefore renumbers the
 * visible column but cannot misdirect an edit of row 3 into row 2.
 *
 * ARRAY SOURCE. how.steps is canonical, and the hook ALWAYS spells how.steps —
 * the read-tolerant / write-canonical split every other How section in the repo
 * uses. A legacy site whose rows live under how.items still RENDERS, but its
 * hooks are not re-pointed at how.items: genericContentSchema declares no
 * how.items list and no fallbackPaths for it, so a hook spelled that way would
 * be inert in the sidebar.
 *
 * NOT RENDERED, deliberately:
 *   - No id on the section. This page's anchors are menu / about / gallery /
 *     visit / faq / main; the design gives HOW-IT-WORKS no id and no nav link
 *     points at it, so inventing #how would add a target nothing aims for.
 *   - No max-width on the headline. The wrapper's .h2 caps itself at 24ch, which
 *     is right for the design's LEFT-aligned headlines; this one is centred by
 *     its parent, and a 24ch block with no auto margins would hug the left edge
 *     while centring its text inside itself. The design states no cap on this
 *     h2, so the cap is removed rather than converted to `margin-inline:auto`.
 *   - No border-left anywhere. The design rules the top of the row, then the
 *     right and bottom of every cell — so the row has a right edge and no left
 *     edge. That asymmetry is drawn, not missed; it is what lets the first step
 *     sit flush with the eyebrow and the headline above it.
 *   - No italic-emphasis rule. The headline is fed with set:html and scoped
 *     rules cannot reach elements set:html creates (R3); the wrapper already
 *     paints `:where(.h2 em)` italic walnut from its global block, so a scoped
 *     duplicate here would be dead CSS.
 *   - No `target="_blank"` on an IN-PAGE action. The design hard-codes one
 *     because its own href is a Messenger URL, but the same button may now
 *     resolve to '#visit', and opening an in-page anchor in a new tab is a bug.
 *     Emitted for http(s) only.
 *   - NO SECTION PADDING OF ITS OWN, and this one was audited BACK to the
 *     wrapper. The design writes `padding: 84px 30px 0` on every section and
 *     lets the NEXT section's top padding be the whole gap; PageBM instead
 *     declares ONE shared symmetric rhythm — `section { padding:
 *     clamp(52px,7vw,84px) 0 }`, capped at the design's own 84px — and that
 *     wrapper rule is the contract all fifteen sections in this variant sit on
 *     (GalleryBM and CredentialsBM state the same, and only TrustBM departs,
 *     because the design gives that strip no padding at all). A top-only
 *     padding here would NOT restore the design's 84px: GalleryBM above still
 *     pays its own symmetric bottom 84, so the space above this row is 168
 *     either way, and the only thing that would change is that HOW becomes the
 *     one section in the variant that butts into the reviews below it. The
 *     horizontal 30px is likewise not restated: .wrap is `min(1160px, 92vw)`,
 *     the design's 1160 cap and its side air in one declaration.
 *   - No star row, no rating, no photo: this section has none in the design.
 *
 * SCOPING (R3). One scoped <style> block, no is:global. The section class is
 * "hbm" and the scoped classes are prefixed hbm- for THIS template: an audit had
 * to rename a set of classes copied verbatim from the previous template, so
 * nothing here reuses HowBL's how-band / how-grid / how-step / how-num names.
 * Button classes are .btn / .btn--fill only — buildOverrideCss pins label
 * colours on .btn-primary / .btn-ghost / .nav-cta / .cta-band .btn / .testi .btn
 * with !important, and none of those selectors reaches anything here (R4).
 */
// `layout` is accepted because PageBM passes it to every section uniformly;
// this block reads nothing outside content.*.
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const how = content.how || {};
const tag = typeof how.tag === 'string' ? how.tag.trim() : '';
// Trimmed, and otherwise untouched: this goes out through set:html, so whatever
// markup the owner typed reaches the wrapper's em rule intact.
const headline = typeof how.headline === 'string' ? how.headline.trim() : '';

type Step = { title?: string; body?: string; desc?: string; description?: string };
// Read tolerantly — a legacy site stores its rows under how.items — and write
// canonically. See ARRAY SOURCE above.
const source: Step[] = Array.isArray(how.steps) ? how.steps : (Array.isArray(how.items) ? how.items : []);
const titleOf = (s: Step): string => String(s?.title ?? '').trim();
const bodyOf = (s: Step): string => String(s?.body ?? s?.desc ?? s?.description ?? '').trim();
// A step the admin added but never filled would otherwise print a walnut numeral
// over nothing inside a fully ruled cell, and a title of pure whitespace would
// ship an empty <h3>. Normalise first, drop the blanks (whitespace counts as
// blank) and keep the ORIGINAL index on every survivor.
const rows = source
  .map((s, i: number) => ({ i, title: titleOf(s), body: bodyOf(s) }))
  .filter((r) => Boolean(r.title || r.body));

// Track count follows the DATA, capped at the design's three — see THE TRACK
// COUNT above. Three or more filled steps compute exactly the design's
// repeat(3, minmax(0,1fr)).
const cols = String(Math.min(Math.max(rows.length, 1), 3));

// Anchor guard (R7). Resolve in-page hrefs against the ids PageBM actually
// renders — menu, about, gallery, visit, faq, main — and map what
// derive-content-defaults emits onto them. Off-page hrefs (tel:, mailto:, http)
// pass straight through untouched. Keep this table in step with the copies in
// the other BM sections.
const PAGE_ANCHORS = new Set(['menu', 'about', 'gallery', 'visit', 'faq', 'main']);
const ANCHOR_ALIAS: Record<string, string> = {
  book: 'main',
  contact: 'visit',
  host: 'about',
  location: 'visit',
  rooms: 'menu',
  services: 'menu',
  why: 'about',
  work: 'gallery',
};
const onPage = (href: string | undefined, fallback: string): string => {
  if (!href) return fallback;
  if (!href.startsWith('#')) return href;
  const id = href.slice(1);
  if (PAGE_ANCHORS.has(id)) return href;
  const alias = ANCHOR_ALIAS[id];
  return alias && PAGE_ANCHORS.has(alias) ? `#${alias}` : fallback;
};

const hero = content.hero || {};
const ctaText = typeof hero.cta1?.text === 'string' ? hero.cta1.text.trim() : '';
// #visit is where this template puts the address, the hours and the way to reach
// the café, so it is the honest landing place for an action that cannot be
// resolved — the step above it has just told the visitor to get in touch.
const ctaHref = onPage(hero.cta1?.href, '#visit');
// Carried for http(s) ONLY — see NOT RENDERED above.
const ctaExternal = /^https?:/i.test(ctaHref);
---
{rows.length > 0 && (
  <section class="hbm">
    <div class="wrap">
      {(tag || headline) && (
        <div class="hbm-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="how.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2 hbm-h2" data-field="how.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      )}

      <ol class="hbm-grid" role="list" style={`--cols:${cols}`}>
        {rows.map((step, k) => (
          <li class="hbm-cell reveal" data-delay={String(Math.min(k, 3))}>
            {/* DERIVED chrome: display index, aria-hidden, no data-field. The
                <ol> already carries the ordinal for assistive tech. */}
            <span class="fig hbm-n" aria-hidden="true">{String(k + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            {step.title && <h3 class="hbm-t" data-field={`how.steps.${step.i}.title`}>{step.title}</h3>}
            {step.body && <p class="body hbm-p" data-field={`how.steps.${step.i}.body`}>{step.body}</p>}
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>

      {ctaText && (
        <div class="hbm-act reveal">
          <a
            class="btn btn--fill"
            href={ctaHref}
            target={ctaExternal ? '_blank' : undefined}
            rel={ctaExternal ? 'noopener noreferrer' : undefined}
            data-field="hero.cta1.text"
            data-href-field="hero.cta1.href"
          >{ctaText}</a>
        </div>
      )}
    </div>
  </section>
)}
<style>
  /* The white ground, the underlined walnut eyebrow, the Libre Caslon headline,
     the italic emphasis, the body copy and both button treatments all arrive
     from the wrapper's shared primitives. This file must not restate any of
     them — restating the eyebrow in particular would cost the design its
     signature underline.

     What IS set here is the block rhythm and the ruled grid.

     THE SECTION STATES NO VERTICAL PADDING AND NO MEASURE. Design: `padding:
     84px 30px 0; max-width:1160px`. Both halves are the wrapper's: `.wrap` is
     `min(1160px, 92vw)`, and `html[data-page="BM"] section` is
     `clamp(52px,7vw,84px) 0`, which tops out at exactly the design's 84px. A
     scoped `.hbm { padding: … 0 0 }` WOULD win that rule on specificity — but
     it would not buy the design's 84px gap, because GalleryBM above keeps its
     own symmetric bottom padding; it would only make HOW the single section in
     the variant with no space under it. See the frontmatter. */

  /* Design: centred header block, margin-bottom 44px. The margin lives on the
     block rather than on the h2, so the gap above the grid is identical whether
     or not the owner typed a headline — and derive-content-defaults ships
     how.tag on every site but NO how.headline, so "eyebrow, then straight into
     the grid" is the DEFAULT state, not an edge case. */
  .hbm-head { text-align: center; margin-bottom: 44px; }

  /* The eyebrow is owner-typed and set in 10px caps at .34em tracking: one
     unbroken word would otherwise be the widest thing on the page (R8). Only
     wrapping is touched — the underline, the tracking and the walnut come from
     the .kick primitive. */
  .hbm-head .kick { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* Design: margin .26em 0 0, and NO max-width. The wrapper's .h2 is a :where()
     rule so both win without !important. The cap has to go rather than become
     `margin-inline:auto`: see NOT RENDERED in the frontmatter. */
  .hbm-h2 { margin: 0.26em 0 0; max-width: none; }

  /* THE GRID IS DRAWN WITH BORDERS, NOT GAPS — the design's rule for this whole
     page. Top hairline on the row, right + bottom hairline on every cell, and
     no gap at all, so the columns share their rules.
     minmax(0,1fr) rather than 1fr so a long unbroken word in a step cannot
     widen its own track and push the page sideways.
     --cols is the design's 3 for any real three-step process and only drops
     below it when the owner filled fewer — an empty track in a grid drawn with
     BORDERS is a hairline hanging over nothing. The inline custom property sets
     no `grid-template-columns` of its own, so the 900px rule below still wins
     on source order. */
  .hbm-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(var(--cols, 3), minmax(0, 1fr));
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }
  /* Design: padding 32px 30px, border-right + border-bottom.
     min-width:0 as well as the minmax() above: a grid item's default min-width
     is auto, so one unbroken word would still widen its track. */
  .hbm-cell {
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 32px 30px;
    border-right: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    list-style: none;
  }

  /* Design: Libre Caslon Text ITALIC, 17px, walnut, on its own line. The face,
     the weight, the walnut and the tabular figures come from the wrapper's .fig,
     which PageBM documents as the primitive a section opts into for a numeral;
     the italic and the size are this section's. `display:block` because the
     design's numeral is a <div> and the title must start on a new line, and the
     leading is restated as `normal` because the design states none on it — .fig
     ships line-height:1, and the h3's 13px top margin was drawn against a
     `normal` box. */
  .hbm-n.fig {
    display: block;
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 17px;
    line-height: normal;
  }

  /* Design: Libre Caslon Text 400, 24px, margin 13px 0 9px, #241B14. The face,
     the weight and the ink all arrive from the wrapper's shared h1-h4 reset —
     unlike the band sections of this family, the ground here is paper, so the
     colour is correct as inherited and is NOT restated. The leading is: the
     design states none above this h3, so it computes to `normal`, while the
     shared reset is 1.14, which would set a two-line title tighter than drawn.
     Qualified with the cell class so it beats that (0,1,1) reset. */
  .hbm-cell .hbm-t {
    font-size: 24px;
    line-height: normal;
    margin: 13px 0 9px;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  /* Qualified with the parent class so it beats the wrapper's bare-element
     paragraph reset. Size, leading and the --ink-soft colour are already exactly
     the design's, via the .body primitive. */
  .hbm-cell p.hbm-p { margin: 0; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }

  /* Design: centred, margin-top 38px. */
  .hbm-act { text-align: center; margin-top: 38px; }

  /* R8 · this section owns its own grid. Three tracks are the design, and they
     go straight to ONE at 900px rather than through a two-up: at 900 each track
     is about 276px less 60px of cell padding, a ~24-character measure, and three
     steps in two tracks leave a half-ruled orphan row that reads as a broken
     grid rather than as an editorial one. Stacked, the paragraph takes a measure
     cap instead: a step should not run to 110 characters just because its column
     is now the whole page. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .hbm-grid { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    /* Stacked, a right-hand rule on a full-width cell is a stray vertical line
       down the edge of the page; the row rules carry the grid on their own. */
    .hbm-cell { border-right: 0; }
    .hbm-cell p.hbm-p { max-width: 62ch; }
    .hbm-head { margin-bottom: 34px; }
  }
  /* 520 · tighten the stack. Nothing here may push the page sideways: the
     overflow-wrap rules above and the minmax/min-width tracks cover the text,
     and the action goes full-bleed below. Every size in this block is BELOW the
     value it replaces, so nothing grows as the viewport narrows. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .hbm-head { margin-bottom: 26px; }
    .hbm-cell { padding: 26px 18px; }
    .hbm-n.fig { font-size: 16px; }
    .hbm-cell .hbm-t { font-size: 21px; margin: 11px 0 8px; }
    .hbm-act { margin-top: 28px; }
    /* Full-bleed action: the wrapper pins .btn to white-space: nowrap, which
       would push the viewport sideways on a long owner-typed label. */
    .hbm-act .btn { width: 100%; white-space: normal; }
  }
</style>
